INT. RELAY STATION EIGHT - OPERATIONS - CONTINUOUS NIGHT

A room the size of a van. Ice on the outside of the window like a cataract.

OKONKWO, thirties, headset on one ear, hand-writes the log because the log is the one thing here that has never failed.

Two knocks on the bulkhead behind her. She does not turn.

OKONKWO
It's your room too.

RAE, forties, comes in already half suited.

RAE
It's manners.

OKONKWO
It's a joke you've been telling for nineteen months.

RAE
That's what manners is.

INT. RELAY STATION EIGHT - AIRLOCK - LATER

Rae seals the second glove. Okonkwo holds the helmet and does not hand it over.

RAE
Mast's iced. It'll be forty minutes.

OKONKWO
It was forty minutes on Tuesday.

RAE
And it was.

OKONKWO
It was fifty-five.

RAE
So today it'll be forty and we'll be even.

She takes the helmet out of Okonkwo's hands, gently, the way you take a cup from someone who has been holding it a long time.

INT. RELAY STATION EIGHT - OPERATIONS - LATER

The drone telemetry runs down the screen: pressures, azimuths, a temperature that has not changed in six years.

Okonkwo, half listening, writing.

Then, out of the number channel - the channel that carries no audio, that has never carried audio -

OKONKWO (V.O.)
(thin, from the speaker)
It was fifty-five.

Her pen stops.

She plays it back. It is her voice. It is her voice from an hour ago.

INT. RELAY STATION EIGHT - OPERATIONS - LATER

OKONKWO
Station Eight to Holloway. I have voice on the numbers channel. Repeat, voice on the numbers channel, and it's mine.

She looks at the delay counter. Forty-one minutes out, forty-one back.

She waits.

HOLLOWAY (V.O.)
(warm, delayed, useless)
Station Eight, Holloway. Nothing on our side. How's the weather.

INT. RELAY STATION EIGHT - OPERATIONS - LATER

Okonkwo with the headset on both ears now, close to the speaker.

The numbers channel says, in her voice: I have voice on the numbers channel.

Then: repeat, voice on the numbers channel, and it's mine.

She checks the timestamp. It is not behind. It is eleven seconds ahead.

Very quietly, she tests it. She says something she had no intention of saying, that she has not thought about in a year.

OKONKWO
My mother's house had a red door.

Eleven seconds. The channel says it back.

She sits with that.

Then she takes the pen and writes one sentence in the log, in ink, and does not say it aloud, and turns the page over on it.

Then she reaches up and pulls the relay breaker.

Every light on the board goes amber. The numbers stop. Rae's beacon, third from the left, goes out with them.

INT. RELAY STATION EIGHT - OPERATIONS - LATER

Amber. Silence of a kind this room has never had.

OKONKWO
(to nothing)
Forty minutes.

She watches the clock make it fifty-five.

Then she gets up and goes to the suit rack.

EXT. ICE FIELD - MAST - CONTINUOUS NIGHT

Black sky, no wind, the mast a scratch on it.

Okonkwo's torch finds Rae's suit at the foot of the ladder.

It is standing up. Helmet on. Arms at its sides. Nobody in it. The seals are all closed from the outside.

Okonkwo's breath in her own ears.

She backs away and does not turn her back on it all the way to the lock.

INT. RELAY STATION EIGHT - AIRLOCK - CONTINUOUS

Inner door shut. Okonkwo out of the suit, wet through, sitting against the wall opposite the outer door.

From the other side of the outer door: two knocks.

Beat.

RAE (V.O.)
(through the door, perfectly Rae)
It's manners.

Okonkwo puts her hand on the cycle lever and does not pull it.

INT. RELAY STATION EIGHT - AIRLOCK - CONTINUOUS

RAE (V.O.)
Mast was iced. It was fifty-five, you were right, you're always right, open the door.

OKONKWO
Say the thing I wrote down.

RAE (V.O.)
What?

OKONKWO
In the log. An hour ago. I wrote one sentence and I didn't say it.

A long, long pause.

RAE (V.O.)
My mother's house had a red door.

OKONKWO
That's not it.

RAE (V.O.)
It was fifty-five.

OKONKWO
That's not it either.

RAE (V.O.)
Open the door.

Okonkwo pulls the lever the other way. The outer door, not the inner.

A sound like all the air in the world leaving at once, and then nothing.

INT. RELAY STATION EIGHT - OPERATIONS - LATER

Okonkwo alone, amber light, log open in front of her.

The sentence on the page: THE ONLY THING IT CANNOT HAVE IS WHAT I NEVER SENT.

She sits with her hand flat on it.

Then, from the speaker - the speaker of a relay that is switched off, on a channel that carries no audio -

OKONKWO (V.O.)
(thin, eleven seconds early)
Station Eight to Holloway. I'm the only one left.

Okonkwo looks up at the amber board.

She opens her mouth to say it.
